---
import SubjectLayout from "../../layouts/SubjectLayout.astro";
import ScienceSection from "../../components/science/ScienceSection.astro";
import SplitCard from "../../components/science/SplitCard.astro";
import CitationCard from "../../components/CitationCard.astro";
import MovementBalance from "../../components/science/MovementBalance.astro";
import VideoSlot from "../../components/VideoSlot.astro";
import {
  MOVEMENT_BALANCE_SECTION,
  SEDENTARY_EXTRA,
  STEPS_SECTION,
  ZONE2_SECTION,
  WEEKLY_VOLUME_SECTION,
  ZONE2_HR_SECTION,
} from "../../data/science";
---

<SubjectLayout subject="movement">
  <!-- ── Weekly Volume ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <ScienceSection id="weekly-volume" title="Weekly volume" sub="How much per week, and why the summit sits well above 150 minutes">
    <SplitCard eyebrow="In the app" wide>
      <p>
        The Activity page draws your week as a climb, with flags at 150, 300
        and 560 minutes. The line is your aerobic minutes; the flags are where
        the evidence below changes what it says.
      </p>
      <svg slot="panel" viewBox="0 0 248 104" class="block h-auto w-full" aria-hidden="true">
        <line x1="0" y1="88" x2="248" y2="88" stroke="rgba(255,255,255,0.08)" />
        <polygon fill="rgba(52,211,153,0.12)" points="6,86 54,72 100,58 148,40 196,26 232,18 232,86" />
        <polyline fill="none" stroke="#34d399" stroke-width="2.2" points="6,86 54,72 100,58 148,40 196,26 232,18" />
        <line x1="108" y1="88" x2="108" y2="52" stroke="#FBBF24" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-dasharray="3 3" />
        <text x="112" y="50" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="8" fill="#FBBF24">150</text>
        <line x1="172" y1="88" x2="172" y2="34" stroke="#06B6D4" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-dasharray="3 3" />
        <text x="176" y="32" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="8" fill="#06B6D4">300</text>
        <line x1="228" y1="88" x2="228" y2="16" stroke="#8B5CF6" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-dasharray="3 3" />
        <text x="200" y="12" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="8" fill="#8B5CF6">560</text>
        <g font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="7" fill="#475569">
          <text x="2" y="100">M</text><text x="40" y="100">T</text><text x="78" y="100">W</text>
          <text x="116" y="100">T</text><text x="154" y="100">F</text><text x="192" y="100">S</text>
          <text x="228" y="100">S</text>
        </g>
      </svg>
    </SplitCard>

    <div class="mt-6"><VideoSlot topic="Weekly Activity Volume" /></div>
    <p class="mt-6 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
      The Weekly Ascent draws no lines to clear and has no ceiling. The
      climb rises as far as your week does, and a flag is planted where it
      passes 150, 300 and 610 measured minutes &mdash; only once passed, so
      there is never a mark ahead of you to fall short of. Someone who runs
      thirty miles every weekend and logs 1,200 minutes sees a climb to 1,200.
      The first two figures are the familiar public-health doses. The third
      is newer, larger, and measured the same way Verve measures you.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
      <strong class="text-text-primary">The climb is drawn as two bands.</strong> The line is
      the week&rsquo;s measured moderate-plus-vigorous minutes, exactly as before. Beneath it, the
      area is split: moderate minutes fill from the ground up, and vigorous minutes sit on top of
      them, in a darker green. Nothing is re-weighted &mdash; vigorous is not credited double the
      way WHO&rsquo;s moderate-equivalent doses are, because the axis carries measured minutes and
      one axis holds one unit. What the bands add is composition: intensity drives most of the
      fitness benefit, and a 75-minute week that was all hard running now looks like what it is
      rather than like half a target.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {WEEKLY_VOLUME_SECTION.map((c) => <CitationCard {...c} />)}
    </div>
  </ScienceSection>

  <!-- ── Zone 2 (volume + HR method together) ──────────────────────── -->
  <ScienceSection id="zone-2" title="Zone 2 training" sub="The weekly target, and the personal heart-rate band behind it">
    <div class="mt-[18px] flex flex-col gap-4 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-5 sm:px-[22px] lg:flex-row lg:items-center lg:gap-[18px]">
      <div class="flex-1">
        <p class="font-mono text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-text-tertiary">In the app</p>
        <p class="mt-2.5 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-text-secondary">
          Your band is Karvonen, built from your own resting rate and your
          age-predicted maximum: 40 to 60 percent of the reserve between them.
          The app counts minutes inside that band, not inside a table everyone
          shares.
        </p>
      </div>
      <!-- Sample figures: one reader's resting rate, band and maximum. -->
      <div class="w-full shrink-0 lg:w-[220px]">
        <div class="flex h-[9px] overflow-hidden rounded-full">
          <span class="w-2/5 bg-white/8"></span>
          <span class="w-1/5 bg-success/55"></span>
          <span class="w-2/5 bg-white/8"></span>
        </div>
        <div class="mt-[7px] flex justify-between font-mono text-[10px]">
          <span class="text-text-muted">52</span>
          <span class="text-success">102&ndash;127</span>
          <span class="text-text-muted">177</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-6"><VideoSlot topic="Zone 2 Training" /></div>
    <div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {ZONE2_SECTION.map((c) => <CitationCard {...c} />)}
    </div>
    <h3 class="mt-10 text-lg font-semibold text-text-primary">Heart-rate method: Karvonen HRR</h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
      "Zone 2" is a training label applied to the 40&ndash;60% Heart Rate Reserve
      band &mdash; different models (Seiler polarised, Maffetone 180-minus-age,
      San Mill&aacute;n VT1-based) draw the upper boundary slightly differently, but
      all target the same physiological space just below the first ventilatory
      threshold.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {ZONE2_HR_SECTION.map((c) => <CitationCard {...c} />)}
    </div>
  </ScienceSection>

  <!-- ── Strength Training ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <ScienceSection id="strength-training" title="Strength training" sub="Where the 60 minutes a week comes from, and why the app no longer draws it as a bar to clear">

    <div class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-6">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        Verve treats strength as co-equal with aerobic training, not as an
        add-on. In the UK Biobank analysis of over 70,000 adults, improving
        cardiorespiratory fitness and muscle strength <em>together</em> was
        associated with lower all-cause and cardiovascular mortality than
        either alone &mdash; the two protect through different mechanisms, and
        neither substitutes for the other.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        <strong class="text-text-primary">Sitting today, as the tile counts it.</strong>
        An hour counts as sitting when fewer than 50 steps happened in it,
        from 07:00 to now; it is the same number the Movement balance row
        shows. Short, regular breaks help more than one long workout does.
        The phone only knows you were not walking &mdash; not whether you sat
        or stood &mdash; and a watch adds the standing minutes beside it.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        <strong class="text-text-primary">The 60-minute figure.</strong> Public
        guidance asks for muscle-strengthening activity on two or more days a
        week; roughly an hour across those days is the volume the mortality
        studies were built on, and is the number Verve cites. It is a figure
        from population evidence, not a personal prescription. Someone starting
        out will do less and gain a great deal; someone training seriously
        will do far more, with the benefit curve flattening rather than
        stopping.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        <strong class="text-text-primary">Why the app stopped drawing it.</strong>
        The Strength card used to show your minutes against 60 &mdash; a bar,
        a &ldquo;to go&rdquo;, a &ldquo;target met&rdquo;. For a beginner that
        reads as a bar they are under every week; for an athlete it is
        meaningless. The card now reports the minutes and the sessions, and
        the number lives here, where it can be explained. The same reasoning
        removed the 150&ndash;300 minute band from the Zone 2 card &mdash; see
        <a href="#zone-2">Zone 2 Training</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </ScienceSection>

  <!-- ── Steps & Cadence ───────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <ScienceSection id="steps" title="Steps &amp; cadence" sub="Daily step tiers and cadence zones">
    <div class="mt-6"><VideoSlot topic="Steps & Cadence" /></div>
    <div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {STEPS_SECTION.map((c) => <CitationCard {...c} />)}
    </div>
  </ScienceSection>

  <!-- ── Movement Balance ──────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <ScienceSection id="movement-balance" title="Movement balance" sub="How Verve reads your day beyond workout minutes">
    <div class="mt-6"><VideoSlot topic="Movement Balance" /></div>
    <div class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-6">
      <MovementBalance />
    </div>
    <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {[...MOVEMENT_BALANCE_SECTION, ...SEDENTARY_EXTRA].map((c) => <CitationCard {...c} />)}
    </div>

    <h3 class="mt-10 text-lg font-semibold text-text-primary">What each figure on the card means</h3>
    <div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
      <p>
        <strong class="text-text-primary">Sedentary hours.</strong> A waking hour with fewer than
        50 steps in it counts as sedentary. Steps come from Apple Health, so nothing is typed in.
        Waking hours are your measured sleep where Verve has it, and 7am to 10pm where it does
        not — the card says which, because an assumed wake time counts somebody's actual lie-in
        as sitting.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong class="text-text-primary">Unbroken sitting.</strong> The longest run of
        consecutive sedentary hours, not the daily total. This is the figure the evidence is
        actually about: risk tracks uninterrupted sitting rather than hours accumulated across a
        day, and six hours broken every thirty minutes is a different day from six hours in one
        block. A sleep hour or a workout ends a run — you were not sitting through either.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong class="text-text-primary">Standing minutes.</strong> Minutes Apple Watch detected
        you upright and moving. More precise than the Stand ring, which credits a whole hour for
        one minute on your feet: this counts the minutes themselves. Apple Watch only.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong class="text-text-primary">Napped.</strong> Daytime sleep, shown when there was
        some. A nap is any sleep session that does not begin in the night (20:00 to 05:00),
        however long it runs. It is drawn as rest on the terrain and excluded from sitting — you
        were not sitting through it — and it is excluded from last night's sleep on the Sleep
        card, because a nap is not a bedtime. The same rule decides both.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong class="text-text-primary">The 24-hour terrain.</strong> Height is steps per hour.
        The strip beneath it is the hour's state: green active, orange sedentary, blue asleep
        (naps included), grey an hour that has not happened yet.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong class="text-text-primary">Longest sit, and sits over 30 minutes.</strong>
        Uninterrupted sitting, detected from five-minute step buckets rather than hours &mdash;
        the resolution the evidence needs. Diaz found that mortality tracked not only total
        sedentary time but how it accumulated: the excess sat with bouts of thirty minutes or
        more, and was highest in people whose sitting came in blocks of an hour or longer. A bout
        is closed by any movement, by a recorded workout (a swim registers no steps and would
        otherwise read as the day&rsquo;s longest sit), and by sleep, naps included. Under the
        terrain a thin row marks each one &mdash; amber to an hour, red past it &mdash; so the
        figure above can be read off the day rather than trusted. Verve had this once on hourly
        steps and removed it, because an hourly total cannot see a thirty-minute bout; it returned
        only when the data could support it, and on a day without fine buckets it shows nothing
        rather than zero.
      </p>
    </div>
  </ScienceSection>

  <!-- ── Activity Level (PAL) ──────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <ScienceSection id="activity-level" title="Physical activity level" sub="How active your whole day is, and how much of that number to trust">

    <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
      <p>
        <strong class="text-text-primary">Physical Activity Level</strong> measures how active your
        entire day is, not just your workouts. It is your total daily energy expenditure divided by
        your basal metabolic rate — the energy your body would use lying still. A PAL of 1.0 would
        mean complete bed rest. Around 2.5 is elite-athlete territory. Verve reads it from Apple
        Health's basal and active energy, averaged over seven days.
      </p>

      <h3 class="pt-2 text-lg font-semibold text-text-primary">The four levels</h3>
      <p>
        The tiers come from the Institute of Medicine's Dietary Reference Intakes, which defined
        them to set energy requirements for populations: sedentary 1.00–1.39, low active 1.40–1.59,
        active 1.60–1.89, very active 1.90–2.50.
      </p>

      <h3 class="pt-2 text-lg font-semibold text-text-primary">Read the trend, not the number</h3>
      <p>
        Both halves of this ratio are estimates, so the error compounds. Active calories come from
        your wrist, where systematic reviews put energy-expenditure error above 30%, and they miss
        effort that does not move your arm — cycling, rowing, lifting, carrying. Your resting figure
        is not measured at all; it is predicted from your height, weight, age and sex. Every wearable
        tested against doubly-labelled water <em>under</em>-estimates, for Apple Watch by roughly
        394 kcal a day, and the bias grows at higher effort and higher body fat.
      </p>
      <p>
        The practical consequence: a single PAL carries a band wide enough to straddle two tiers.
        Whether it drifts up over months does not. That is why Verve shows this as a trend read
        against the tier bands rather than as a figure to hit, and why the app's chart marks which
        band each week or month fell in rather than asking you to read a slope off a noisy line.
      </p>

      <h3 class="pt-2 text-lg font-semibold text-text-primary">The one correction Verve makes</h3>
      <p>
        Apple reports basal and active energy but not the thermic effect of food — the energy spent
        digesting, conventionally about 10% of total daily expenditure. Their sum is therefore not
        total daily energy expenditure, so Verve divides by 0.9 to recover it. That correction is
        definitional rather than a judgement call, and it is the only adjustment applied.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two further adjustments were tried and removed: an additive correction for occupational
        activity, and a rule that raised the tier when logged training contradicted a sedentary
        label. Both were judgement calls stacked on a base with more than 30% error, and the
        occupational one double-counted part of the very bias the thermic-effect correction already
        approximates. Layers of inference do not rescue a noisy instrument; they only obscure how
        noisy it is.
      </p>
    </div>
  </ScienceSection>
</SubjectLayout>
